WEBVTT

0
00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:04.650
Welcome back.

1
00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.229
We have established that AI requires extremely

2
00:00:08.229 --> 00:00:11.270
tight coordination within a single data center.

3
00:00:12.569 --> 00:00:14.060
But what happens when you split

4
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.170
that workload across different geographic regions,

5
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:21.100
it becomes a massive architectural challenge.

6
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.250
Running training or inference across multiple regions

7
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.780
significantly increases coordination overhead.

8
00:00:30.399 --> 00:00:33.259
Let us look at a cross-region training scenario.

9
00:00:35.029 --> 00:00:39.009
You might have your data preprocessing happening in region A

10
00:00:39.189 --> 00:00:42.270
and your heavy GPU compute in region B.

11
00:00:43.889 --> 00:00:49.200
The physical distance between these regions introduces hard latency floors.

12
00:00:50.529 --> 00:00:53.049
You cannot bypass the speed of light.

13
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.090
If the network link between region A and region

14
00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.950
B drops even a fraction of its bandwidth,

15
00:01:01.169 --> 00:01:03.189
the GPUs in region B

16
00:01:03.319 --> 00:01:06.410
can become underutilized while waiting for data.

17
00:01:07.269 --> 00:01:11.360
They sit idle waiting for data to cross the geographic divide,

18
00:01:11.709 --> 00:01:14.690
instantly destroying your compute efficiency.

19
00:01:15.779 --> 00:01:19.639
Inference introduces a different set of boundary issues.

20
00:01:20.519 --> 00:01:23.709
When coordinating a global inference architecture,

21
00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.559
you must maintain model consistency

22
00:01:26.559 --> 00:01:30.339
across all physical locations simultaneously.

23
00:01:30.639 --> 00:01:33.059
If a user query hits region A,

24
00:01:33.239 --> 00:01:36.400
or region A is slightly out of sync with region B,

25
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:42.349
The system may produce inconsistent behavior depending on the synchronization

26
00:01:42.559 --> 00:01:43.690
and update timing.

27
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:49.050
Your coordination mechanisms must actively force consistency

28
00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.089
across thousands of miles.

29
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:57.720
Geographic boundaries amplify every single timing issue we discussed earlier.

30
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.760
You are no longer just managing a network,

31
00:02:02.019 --> 00:02:04.419
you are managing a globally coordinated,

32
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:05.800
distributed system.